  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 AM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nCurrent H5 analysis shows the upper low centered over the Front\nRange, with enhanced southwesterly mid/upper level flow \ndownstream across Kansas and Missouri. At the surface, the \naccompanying surface cyclone is currently lifting to the \nnortheast out of northern Oklahoma. This has helped generate a \nlarge shield of showers and thunderstorms across \neastern/southeastern Kansas, lifting to the northeast into our \nCWA. The surface low should continue to lift northeast through \nthe morning hours, pulling a warm front south to north through \nmuch of the CWA and allowing for temperatures to warm into the \nupper 60s to mid 70s for most of the area by early afternoon. \nScattered showers with perhaps a few embedded thunderstorms are \nlikely through the remainder of the morning hours and into early\nafternoon. However, expected rainfall totals have trended \nsignificantly downward, and the remaining flood watch in effect \nacross far southern portions of the CWA has been canceled. \n\nThe closed mid/upper low across the Front Range is progged to \nbecome an open wave and eject into the central CONUS by later \ntoday, which will push the surface low and trailing cold front \nthrough the CWA from west to east this afternoon. Winds will \nturn northwesterly behind the front, increasing to 15 to 20 mph \nwith gusts up to 30 mph. Colder temperatures and drier air will \nalso move in behind the front. Another mid/upper trough will \nbecome more amplified and dig further south out of Canada and \ninto the Midwest late tonight into early Friday, sending another\nreinforcing shot of colder and drier air, with temperatures \nfalling into the mid 40s by Friday morning. With gusty \nnorthwesterly winds continuing, wind chills Friday morning \nshould fall into the mid to upper 30s. With strong cold air \nadvection on Friday, temperatures will struggle to warm, and \nguidance has continued to trend colder with max temps Friday now\nonly projected to reach the lower to mid 50s (although high res\nguidance suggests even colder temps than this). Gusty north \nnorthwesterly winds will continue through mid to late afternoon.\nThe pressure gradient should begin to subside by Friday evening\nas a 1036 mb surface high slides eastward out of the central \nHigh Plains and toward our CWA. By Friday night, the surface \nhigh should be centered directly overhead, yielding calm winds, \nclear skies, and low dew points. This will make for excellent \nradiational cooling conditions, and temperatures early Saturday \nmorning should fall into the mid to upper 30s across most of the\nregion, with temperatures potentially flirting with the \nfreezing mark across portions of northern Missouri and \nnortheastern Kansas. This should yield areas of frost across the\narea, with some sort of frost/freeze product potentially being \nneeded depending on how guidance trends over the next 36 hours. \n\nSaturday should be a beautiful (albeit slightly cool) mid \nOctober day, with highs in the upper 50s to lower 60s, clear \nskies, and light winds. Mid level ridging begins to build in \nfrom the west by Sunday and into next week out initially out \nahead of a shortwave trough over Baja California and then out \nahead of a longwave trough that is progged to develop off the \nWest Coast by Monday/Tuesday. This should bring in warmer \ntemperatures and increased moisture, with highs back into the \nlow to mid 70s across most of the CWA by next Monday afternoon \nand mid to even upper 70s by Tuesday.",
